{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'rg -n -i \"(color|colour|#ff|orange)\" docs/source/style.rst docs/source/markup.rst | head -80'","aggregated_output":"docs/source/markup.rst:6:Rich supports a simple markup which you can use to insert color and styles virtually everywhere Rich would accept a string (e.g. :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print` and :meth:`~rich.console.Console.log`).\ndocs/source/markup.rst:89:Some emojis have two variants, the \"emoji\" variant displays in full color, and the \"text\" variant displays in monochrome (whatever your default colors are set to). You can specify the variant you want by adding either `\"-emoji\"` or `\"-text\"` to the emoji code. Here's an example:\ndocs/source/style.rst:7:In various places in the Rich API you can set a \"style\" which defines the color of the text and various attributes such as bold, italic etc. A style may be given as a string containing a *style definition* or as an instance of a :class:`~rich.style.Style` class.\ndocs/source/style.rst:13:A style definition is a string containing one or more words to set colors and attributes.\ndocs/source/style.rst:15:To specify a foreground color use one of the 256 :ref:`appendix-colors`. For example, to print \"Hello\" in magenta::\ndocs/source/style.rst:19:You may also use the color's number (an integer between 0 and 255) with the syntax ``\"color(<number>)\"``. The following will give the equivalent output::\ndocs/source/style.rst:21:    console.print(\"Hello\", style=\"color(5)\")\ndocs/source/style.rst:23:Alternatively you can use a CSS-like syntax to specify a color with a \"#\" followed by three pairs of hex characters, or in RGB form with three decimal integers. The following two lines both print \"Hello\" in the same color (purple)::\ndocs/source/style.rst:28:The hex and rgb forms allow you to select from the full *truecolor* set of 16.7 million colors.\ndocs/source/style.rst:31:    Some terminals only support 256 colors. Rich will attempt to pick the closest color it can if your color isn't available.\ndocs/source/style.rst:33:By itself, a color will change the *foreground* color. To specify a *background* color, precede the color with the word \"on\". For example, the following prints text in red on a white background::\ndocs/source/style.rst:37:You can also set a color with the word ``\"default\"`` which will reset the color to a default managed by your terminal software. This works for backgrounds as well, so the style of ``\"default on default\"`` is what your terminal starts with.\ndocs/source/style.rst:46:* ``\"reverse\"`` or ``\"r\"`` for text with foreground and background colors reversed.\ndocs/source/style.rst:57:Style attributes and colors may be used in combination with each other. For example::\ndocs/source/style.rst:84:    danger_style = Style(color=\"red\", blink=True, bold=True)\ndocs/source/style.rst:100:    style = Style(color=\"magenta\", bgcolor=\"yellow\", italic=True)\ndocs/source/style.rst:109:If you reuse styles it can be a maintenance headache if you ever want to modify an attribute or color -- you would have to change every line where the style is used. Rich provides a :class:`~rich.theme.Theme` class which you can use to define custom styles that you can refer to by name. That way you only need to update your styles in one place.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
